# Declaring a function

`fn` declares a named function. A declaration gives the name, the parameters
with their types, the result type after `:`, and the body:

```hql
fn days(pages: Int, per_day: Int) : Int = (pages + per_day - 1) / per_day
[days(412, 40), days(280, 40)]
```

```text
[11, 7]
```

`days` has the type `(Int, Int) -> Int`. The parameter types and the result
type are always written. The checker does not infer them from the body,
because the declaration is the contract that callers rely on.

A call must pass every parameter, with the right type:

```hql
fn days(pages: Int, per_day: Int) : Int = (pages + per_day - 1) / per_day
days(412)
```

```text
check error[type-mismatch]: this function takes 2 arguments, not 1
 --> <expression>:2:1
  |
2 | days(412)
  | ^^^^^^^^^
```

The body after `=` is one expression. A longer body is a block in braces;
[Function bodies](bodies.hmd) covers both forms.

## Parameters have no defaults

A function you declare in HQL takes every argument it declares. A parameter
cannot have a default value, so `fn per_week(per_day: Int = 40)` is
rejected. Some functions in the standard library do have defaults, such as
`limit: Int = 0`; those are declared in HQL and implemented in Rust, as
[[32-functions-and-declarations|chapter 32]] shows. When an argument is
usually the same, bind the value to a name and pass the name.

## Recursion

A function's name is bound before its body is checked, so the body can call
the function itself:

```hql
fn factorial(n: Int) : Int = 1 given n <= 1 otherwise n * factorial(n - 1)
[1, 2, 3, 4, 5] | map(factorial)
```

```text
[1, 2, 6, 24, 120]
```

`map(factorial)` passes the function by its name. A declared function is a
value like a lambda.

## Functions as parameters and results

A parameter can have a function type, and so can the result. `adder` returns
a function, and `twice` takes one:

```hql
fn adder(k: Int) : Int -> Int = n => n + k
fn twice(f: Int -> Int, x: Int) : Int = f(f(x))
twice(adder(3), 30)
```

```text
36
```

The function `adder(3)` returns keeps the value of `k` it was created with.
A function that keeps values from where it was created is a **closure**.

## Variadic parameters

A last parameter written `...T` accepts any number of trailing arguments, and
the body sees them as a list:

```hql
fn total(xs: ...Int) : Int = xs | sum
[total(), total(1, 2), total(1, 2, 3, 4)]
```

```text
[0, 3, 10]
```

## A result that may be missing

A function that may have no result returns an `Option<T>`. `some(x)` holds a
value and `none` holds nothing:

```hql
fn rating_of(r: Int) : Option<Int> = some(r) given r > 0 otherwise none
[5, 0, 4] | map(rating_of) | somes
```

```text
[5, 4]
```

`somes` keeps the present values. [[02-data-types/option|Option]] covers the
other ways to handle an `Option`.
